  {
   "s": 0.0,
   "e": 3.68,
   "text": "If you save whatever's left at the end of the month, you'll probably save nothing."
  },
  {
   "s": 4.06,
   "e": 8.7,
   "text": "Because there's always something. A dinner out a sale a bill you forgot about and by the thirtieth"
  },
  {
   "s": 8.7,
   "e": 13.92,
   "text": "it's gone without one big mistake. So flip the order, save first on the day you get paid and"
  },
  {
   "s": 13.92,
   "e": 19.04,
   "text": "then spend what's left. Say you want five thousand dollars in a year, that's about four hundred and"
  },
  {
   "s": 19.04,
   "e": 24.28,
   "text": "seventeen dollars a month. The trick is to make it automatic. Set up a transfer for the day after"
  },
  {
   "s": 24.28,
   "e": 29.28,
   "text": "payday into a separate savings account so it never sits in your spending money. Can't do 400"
  },
  {
   "s": 29.28,
   "e": 33.68,
   "text": "yet start with a hundred. The amount can grow later. Paying yourself first is the habit that"
  },
  {
   "s": 33.68,
